# Settings for the copy of the backups elsewhere and for the monitoring pings, at /opt/poketopia/ops.env. Only root can
# read it (setup.sh creates it from this file with mode 600): the deploy user, which GitHub's deploy key logs in as,
# must not be able to reach or delete the copies elsewhere. Never put the real file in the repository.
#
# systemd reads it for poketopia-backup.service and poketopia-disk-check.service, and offsite.sh for restic by hand.
# Plain KEY=value lines; put a value with spaces in double quotes. Everything is optional: empty switches it off.

# --- The copy of the backups elsewhere (restic) ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# After the daily backup, backup.sh sends that day's database dump and image folder to a restic repository somewhere
# else, encrypted on this server before they leave it, and keeps 30 days there. Pick storage in the EU (the privacy
# policy), for example an S3-compatible bucket or an SFTP storage box. Then, as root:
#   1. fill in RESTIC_REPOSITORY, the provider's login below, and a long random password in RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE:
#        openssl rand -base64 32 > /root/restic-password && chmod 600 /root/restic-password
#   2. /opt/poketopia/bin/offsite.sh init
#   3. keep a copy of that password somewhere safe outside this server (a password manager): without it the copy
#      elsewhere can't be read, and it is lost with the server.
# Examples of a repository (see restic's documentation for the exact form your provider needs):
#   s3:https://<endpoint of the provider>/<bucket>/poketopia
#   sftp:<user>@<host>:poketopia
RESTIC_REPOSITORY=
RESTIC_PASSWORD_FILE=/root/restic-password
# The login of the storage. For S3-compatible storage:
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# How long the copy elsewhere keeps backups, in restic's form (30d is 30 days).
BACKUP_OFFSITE_KEEP=30d

# --- Monitoring pings -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A "heartbeat" or "cron job" check at a monitoring service (one with email alerts, for example healthchecks.io or
# Better Stack): the server calls the URL when all is well and <URL>/fail when it isn't, and the service mails you
# when a ping says so or doesn't come at all, also when the whole server is down.
# After every daily backup (set the check's period to 1 day, with a few hours' grace):
BACKUP_PING_URL=
# Every hour, while every disk the shop uses is below DISK_WARN_PERCENT (period 1 hour, grace 1 hour):
DISK_PING_URL=
DISK_WARN_PERCENT=80
